5. Use ChatGPT or Copilot to generate a checklist of at least 5 best practices for securing a Linux server, and compare it with your own server configuration to identify any missing steps.
=>Disable password authentication and use SSH keys only (with strong key types like Ed25519).
Configure a host firewall (UFW/firewalld) that allows only required ports (e.g., 22, 80/443) and denies everything else by default.
Keep the system updated and remove unnecessary packages/services.
Enable and properly configure mandatory access control (SELinux in Enforcing mode or AppArmor).
Implement regular automated backups of critical data with off-site or versioned storage.
Disable root login over SSH or restrict it to keys only; use a non-privileged user + sudo.
Monitor logs and set up intrusion detection (fail2ban, auditd, etc.).
Use strong file permissions and principle of least privilege.
